\section{Latency, Throughput, and Hazards}

An instruction scheduler needs both semantic ordering constraints
and a model of machine timing. The dependence graph has nodes for
instruction instances and edges $i\to j$ labeled by a minimum issue
distance $\ell_{ij}$, so issue cycles must satisfy
$s_j\ge s_i+\ell_{ij}$. A read of a loaded register requires the
producer's result, whereas a write to an already used physical
register must respect the earlier read even if there is no flow
of data between the instructions. Add write--write and read--write
edges where names or hardware units are reused, and preserve memory
order where accesses may alias and a reordering could change
behavior. Where equal issue cycles are allowed, the target must
specify any ordering within a bundle needed to satisfy the edges.

The distance until a result can be read is not the number of cycles
for which its unit refuses another instruction. A load can have
three-cycle latency on a unit that accepts one load every cycle.
Conversely, an operation may reserve a unit for multiple cycles.
Gibbons and Muchnick's \emph{Efficient Instruction Scheduling for
a Pipelined Architecture} reorganizes already generated code to
reduce interlocks on a particular machine. An interlocked processor
can enforce some timing constraints by stalling; on an exposed
pipeline the corresponding restrictions may be necessary for correct
operation, not just for speed.

\section{A Schedule Derived by Hand}

List scheduling maintains unscheduled instructions with all
predecessors placed, then filters them for operand readiness and
resource availability at the current cycle. A common priority is
the longest remaining dependence path to completion: for a terminal
instruction use its completion latency, and otherwise take a
maximum of edge distance plus successor priority (also allowing
for the instruction's own completion). High priority does not
override the issue test. If no instruction can issue, advance time;
the machine decides whether to encode idle cycles as empty slots
or to rely on hardware interlocks. Update the ready list within
a cycle if the machine permits ordered, same-cycle dependences;
otherwise a ready check deferred to the next cycle can silently
rule out legal packets. A terminator remains constrained by the
region's control-flow contract even if its operands are ready early.

Consider a two-wide target with independent load, multiply, and
add units. Load $L$ produces a result after three cycles. Each of
two independent multiplies $M,N$ also produces its result after
three cycles but reserves the sole multiply unit for its issue
cycle and the next. Add $F$ depends on $L$ and $M$; add $G$ depends
on $N$. Adds finish one cycle after issue. If $M$ wins the tie
against $N$, the list schedule is
\[
  \begin{array}{c|cccccc}
     \text{issue cycle} & 0 & 1 & 2 & 3 & 4 & 5\\ \hline
     \text{operations} & L,M & \text{idle} & N & F & \text{idle} & G
  \end{array}
\]
Completion is at cycle 6. The dependence-only bound is four
cycles, but the later multiply cannot issue before cycle 2 and
then has a four-cycle path to completion. This resource argument
proves optimality for the example. A schedule assigning $N$
to cycle 1 passes every data-readiness test and is still illegal
under the reservation table.

\section{Recognizing Resources and Choosing Priorities}

Bala and Rubin's \emph{Efficient Instruction Scheduling Using
Finite State Automata} represents constructs such as reservations
by automaton state and transitions. For the multiply unit above,
issue in a free state sets a current reservation and a reservation
one cycle ahead. Another multiply in the same packet is rejected;
after one cycle advance the current reservation is still set,
and after a second advance it is gone. An automaton can encode
these transitions in tables instead of checking all earlier
instructions for conflicts, while separate dependency bookkeeping
still controls when $F$ or $G$ may read a result. Test the
packet issue and cycle-advance transitions against a direct
reservation-table simulation.

Malik and colleagues' \emph{Learning Heuristics for Basic Block
Instruction Scheduling} learns a priority heuristic from optimal
schedules. That changes which ready instruction is preferred, not
which machine orderings are allowed. A useful comparison evaluates
schedule length, compile time, and final code after allocation
against a critical-path baseline, using identical dependence and
resource models. Chapter~\ref{ch:ext-238} explains why winning on
schedule length before allocation may still lose after it.

